  "query_preview": "..\nAt the start of the long term period, Wednesday, a surface high \npressure over the Gulf of Alaska will weaken some through the week\nbut remain over the central gulf. This will keep a the weather \nover the panhandle relatively benign with not much wind along with\nprecip. The coastal locations from Yakutat to Sitka and over the \nIcy Strait area may see some chances of light rainfall on \nWednesday with chances persisting over the coast on Thursday from \npersistent onshore flow from the west. The central and southern \narea will see mostly dry conditions with party cloudy to mostly \nclear skies with the northwest winds at the mid levels. Going into\nFriday and next Saturday, southwest flow will increase at the \nupper and mid levels to increase the chances of cloud cover and \nperiod of light rain. \n\nWind wise...they will be mainly on the lighter side with not much\nof a pressure gradient over the inner channels. There will be \nafternoon increases in Northern Lynn canal from the thermal tough \nstrengthening in the Yukon to increase the winds to be 15 to 20 kt\non Wednesday afternoon and then again on Thursday. Over the \neastern gulf with the high pressure drifting to the west the NW \nwinds will increase over the eastern gulf to be 15 to 20kt with it\nbeing close to small craft next Dixon Entrance. \n\nOverall there is above average forecaster confidence in the long \nrange period.",
